\section{Introduction}\label{sec:path-context-intro}

Standard dataflow and abstract interpretation analyses compute a single abstract state at each program point, merging information from all incoming paths. This \emph{path-insensitive} approach can lose precision when different paths lead to contradictory information. \emph{Path-sensitive} analyses maintain separate abstract states for different execution paths, preserving precision at the cost of increased complexity. Similarly, \emph{context-sensitive} analyses distinguish different calling contexts for procedures.

This chapter develops the theory and practice of path-sensitive and context-sensitive analysis, including predicate abstraction, CEGAR (counterexample-guided abstraction refinement), and modern tools that implement these techniques at industrial scale.

\section{Path Sensitivity: Predicate Abstraction}\label{sec:predicate-abstraction}

\subsection{The Idea of Predicate Abstraction}\label{sec:predicate-idea}

\emph{Predicate abstraction} represents the program state by the truth values of a fixed set of \emph{predicates} (boolean expressions over program variables).

\begin{definition}[Predicate Abstraction]\label{def:predicate-abs}
Given a set of predicates $\{p_1, p_2, \ldots, p_k\}$ over program variables, the \textbf{predicate abstraction} of a concrete state $\sigma$ is the boolean vector $(b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_k)$ where $b_i = \text{true}$ iff $\sigma \models p_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Predicate Abstraction]\label{ex:predicate-abs}
With predicates $\{x > 0, y > x, z = 0\}$:
\begin{itemize}
  \item State $\{x = 5, y = 10, z = 0\}$ maps to $(T, T, T)$
  \item State $\{x = -1, y = 3, z = 1\}$ maps to $(F, T, F)$
  \item State $\{x = 5, y = 3, z = 0\}$ maps to $(T, F, T)$
\end{itemize}
\end{example}

\subsection{Boolean Programs}\label{sec:boolean-programs}

\begin{definition}[Boolean Program]\label{def:boolean-program}
Given a program $P$ and a set of predicates $\{p_1, \ldots, p_k\}$, the \textbf{boolean program} $\mathit{BP}(P, \{p_i\})$ is an abstract program over boolean variables $b_1, \ldots, b_k$ that simulates $P$ at the level of the predicates.
\end{definition}

The boolean program is computed by \emph{weakest precondition} computation: for each statement $s$ and predicate $p_i$, we compute $\mathit{wp}(s, p_i)$ and express it as a boolean function of the predicates.

\begin{definition}[Weakest Precondition for Predicates]\label{def:wp-pred}
For a statement $s$ and predicate $p$:
\[
\mathit{wp}(s, p) = \text{the condition on the pre-state such that } p \text{ holds after } s
\]
For example, for $s: x = x + 1$ and $p: x > 0$: $\mathit{wp}(x = x + 1, x > 0) = (x + 1 > 0) = (x > -1)$.
\end{definition}

\section{Abstract Interpretation with Refinement}\label{sec:ai-refinement}

Predicate abstraction can be viewed as a form of abstract interpretation where the abstract domain is the boolean lattice $2^{\{p_1, \ldots, p_k\}}$. The key challenge is choosing the right set of predicates.

\begin{definition}[Predicate Discovery]\label{def:predicate-discovery}
\textbf{Predicate discovery} is the process of finding predicates that are precise enough to prove a property but not so numerous as to make the analysis intractable.
\end{definition}

\subsection{CEGAR: Counterexample-Guided Abstraction Refinement}\label{sec:cegar}

\emph{CEGAR} is an iterative approach that starts with a coarse abstraction and refines it based on spurious counterexamples.

\begin{definition}[CEGAR Loop]\label{def:cegar}
The \textbf{CEGAR loop} consists of:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Abstract}: Build an abstract model with the current set of predicates.
  \item \textbf{Verify}: Check the property on the abstract model.
  \item \textbf{Validate}: If the property holds, it holds on the concrete program (sound). If a counterexample is found, check if it is \emph{spurious} (an artifact of the abstraction).
  \item \textbf{Refine}: If the counterexample is spurious, add predicates to distinguish the spurious path and repeat.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{CEGAR Algorithm}
\label{alg:cegar}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require Program $P$, property $\varphi$
\Ensure $\varphi$ holds on $P$, or a concrete counterexample
\State $\Pi \gets$ initial set of predicates (possibly empty)
\Loop
  \State $A \gets$ build abstract model from $P$ using $\Pi$
  \State $\mathit{result} \gets$ model-check $\varphi$ on $A$
  \If{$\mathit{result} = \text{true}$}
    \State \Return ``Property holds''
  \ElsIf{$\mathit{result}$ is a counterexample $\pi$}
    \State $\mathit{feasible} \gets$ check if $\pi$ is feasible in $P$
    \If{$\mathit{feasible}$}
      \State \Return ``Counterexample: $\pi$''
    \Else
      \State $\Pi \gets \Pi \cup \mathit{refine}(\pi)$ \Comment{Add discriminating predicates}
    \EndIf
  \EndIf
\EndLoop
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Spurious Counterexample Analysis}\label{sec:spurious-analysis}

A counterexample is \emph{spurious} if it does not correspond to any actual execution of the concrete program. Detecting spurious counterexamples requires checking the feasibility of the abstract path.

\begin{definition}[Path Feasibility]\label{def:path-feasibility}
An abstract path $\pi = s_1, s_2, \ldots, s_n$ is \textbf{feasible} if there exists a concrete execution that follows this path. This is checked by computing the weakest precondition of the path condition:
\[
\mathit{wp}(\pi, \text{true}) = \mathit{wp}(s_1, \mathit{wp}(s_2, \ldots \mathit{wp}(s_n, \text{true})\ldots))
\]
If $\mathit{wp}(\pi, \text{true})$ is satisfiable, the path is feasible.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Interpolation-Based Refinement}\label{sec:interpolation}

\emph{Craig interpolation} provides a systematic method for finding refinement predicates.

\begin{definition}[Craig Interpolant]\label{def:interpolant}
Given an unsatisfiable formula $A \wedge B$, a \textbf{Craig interpolant} is a formula $I$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
  \item $A \implies I$
  \item $I \wedge B$ is unsatisfiable
  \item $I$ contains only variables common to $A$ and $B$
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

For a spurious counterexample, the path is split at each program point into a prefix $A$ and a suffix $B$. The interpolants at each split point provide the refinement predicates.

\section{Context Sensitivity for Interprocedural Analysis}\label{sec:context-sensitivity}

Context sensitivity distinguishes different calling contexts for procedures, preventing the conflation of information from unrelated call sites.

\subsection{Inlining-Based Context Sensitivity}\label{sec:inlining-cs}

The simplest form of context sensitivity is \emph{inlining}: replacing each call site with a copy of the called procedure's body. This naturally distinguishes calling contexts but can cause code explosion.

\begin{definition}[Bounded Inlining]\label{def:bounded-inline}
\textbf{Bounded inlining} inlines procedure calls up to a depth $k$. Calls at depth greater than $k$ are treated as context-insensitive.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Cloning-Based Context Sensitivity}\label{sec:cloning-cs}

\begin{definition}[Procedure Cloning]\label{def:proc-cloning}
\textbf{Procedure cloning} creates separate copies of a procedure for each distinct calling context. Each clone is analyzed independently with the specific information from its calling context.
\end{definition}

\section{Combination of Path and Context Sensitivity}\label{sec:combined-sensitivity}

Combining path and context sensitivity provides the highest precision but at significant computational cost.

\begin{definition}[Path-Context Sensitive Analysis]\label{def:path-context}
A \textbf{path-context sensitive} analysis maintains separate abstract states for each combination of execution path and calling context. The abstract state space is the product of the path domain and the context domain.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Trace Partitioning}\label{sec:trace-partitioning}

\begin{definition}[Trace Partitioning]\label{def:trace-partition}
\textbf{Trace partitioning} divides the set of execution traces into equivalence classes and analyzes each class separately. The partition can be based on:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Path conditions (which branches were taken)
  \item Calling context (which call sites were traversed)
  \item Both
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Bounding Trace Partitions with an Overflow Bucket}

Trace partitioning needs a policy for the first path that exceeds the memory budget. Simply dropping the new path is unsound. Evicting old keys and repeatedly recreating them can also make a fixpoint solver do far more work than the nominal budget suggests. One option is to reserve a bucket for all keys outside a fixed whitelist.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} For each program point choose up to $K$ distinguished keys, such as branch histories or a pair of branch history and calling context. Fix the choice before the analysis run. The underlying abstract domain provides a sound join and edge transfer. A transfer out of the overflow bucket may include more behavior than a transfer based on a precise key; it must account for all keys represented there.

\begin{sudocode}[Route states into bounded partitions]
\Require Edge $e:u\to v$, source bucket $b$, state $s$
\Ensure Successor buckets cover all transferred states
\ForAll{sound successor pairs $(q,t)$ from $(e,b,s)$}
  \If{$q$ is unknown}
    \Let{B}{\text{all buckets at }v} \Comment{Include precise keys}
  \ElsIf{$q$ is whitelisted at $v$}
    \Let{B}{\{q\}}
  \Else
    \Let{B}{\{*\}} \Comment{A known unlisted key}
  \EndIf
  \ForAll{$k\in B$}
    \Let{z}{X[v,k]\sqcup t}
    \If{$z\ne X[v,k]$}
      \Let{X[v,k]}{z}
      \State Enqueue $(v,k)$
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
\EndFor
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} A join distinguishes only the key \texttt{fast}. Suppose paths keyed \texttt{fast}, \texttt{slow1}, and \texttt{slow2} arrive with $x=2$, $x=8$, and $x=12$, respectively. The interval for \texttt{fast} is $[2,2]$; the overflow interval is $[8,12]$. An arrival with $x=10$ and no known key expands the \texttt{fast} interval to $[2,10]$ and leaves overflow unchanged. If a new branch determines the successor key on every represented execution, precise routing is again possible.

\textbf{Invariant.} Each trace with a whitelisted key is covered by its exact bucket; other traces are covered by overflow. Buckets are initialized to bottom and are never evicted, so there are at most $K+1$ values per program point. The bound controls the number of values, not the height of the domain; an interval analysis may still need widening. A poor whitelist can merge precisely the paths an assertion needs to distinguish.

\section{Tools: Astr\'ee, ELINA, Infer}\label{sec:tools}

\subsection{Astr\'ee}\label{sec:astree}

\emph{Astr\'ee}~\cite{cousot2003astree} is a static analyzer for C programs developed at \'Ecole Normale Sup\'erieure. It uses abstract interpretation with:
\begin{itemize}
  \item A combination of numerical abstract domains (intervals, octagons)
  \item Sophisticated widening with thresholds
  \item Trace partitioning for path sensitivity
  \item Industrial-scale applications (avionics software)
\end{itemize}

Astr\'ee has been used to prove the absence of runtime errors in critical avionics software, demonstrating that abstract interpretation can scale to industrial applications.

\subsection{ELINA}\label{sec:elina}

\emph{ELINA} (ETH Library for Abstract Interpretation) is a high-performance library for numerical abstract domains. It provides optimized implementations of:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Intervals, octagons, and polyhedra
  \item Template polyhedra
  \item Zoned abstract domains
  \item Zones with congruences
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Infer}\label{sec:infer}

\emph{Infer}~\cite{calcagno2015infer} is a static analysis tool developed at Facebook (Meta) for detecting bugs in large-scale mobile and web applications. It uses:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \emph{Bi-abduction}: A technique for compositional reasoning about heap-manipulating programs
  \item \emph{Iterative analysis}: Combining summary-based analysis with refinement
  \item \emph{Pulse}: A memory safety analyzer based on abstract interpretation
\end{itemize}

Infer analyzes millions of lines of code and has been integrated into the development workflow of major mobile applications.

